    {
      "section_id": "4-1.1",
      "title": "Scope",
      "summary": "Defines the scope of Chapter 4: analysis of stress conditions within the ground including stress at a point, changes in stress from soil and structural loads, empirical methods for loads on buried pipes/conduits/shafts/tunnels, and numerical methods such as finite element analysis.",
      "body": "This chapter describes the analysis of stress conditions within the ground, including stress at a point, changes in stress caused by the application of soil and structural loads, and empirical methods for estimating loads on buried pipes, conduits, shafts, and tunnels. The calculation of stresses and changes in stress using numerical methods, such as the finite element method, is also discussed.",
      "key_points": [
        "Chapter covers stress at a point, stress changes from applied loads, and empirical load methods for buried structures.",
        "Numerical methods (e.g., finite element method) for stress calculation are also discussed.",
        "Scope includes both surface and subsurface loading scenarios."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when an engineer needs to understand what topics and methods are addressed in Chapter 4 before selecting an appropriate analysis approach."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-1.2",
      "title": "State of Stress",
      "summary": "Distinguishes between elastic and plastic stress analysis for soils. Elastic solutions apply when shear stresses are well below shear strength (< ~1/3 of ultimate); most methods in Chapter 4 assume elastic conditions. Plastic solutions assume full shear strength mobilization and apply to slope stability, bearing capacity, and lateral earth pressure problems.",
      "body": "The state of stress within the ground can be analyzed assuming that either elastic or plastic conditions prevail. Elastic solutions are most appropriate for cases in which the shear stresses throughout the soil mass are significantly below the shear strength of the soil and shear failure is not likely. If the shear stress in the soil is less than about one-third of the ultimate shear strength, the stresses within the soil mass will be roughly equal to values calculated from elastic theory (Davis and Selvadurai 1996). The stress conditions calculated using most of the methods in this chapter assume that elastic conditions prevail.\n\nPlastic solutions assume full mobilization of the soil's shear strength within a soil mass or along a specified failure surface. Plastic equilibrium is used for problems, such as slope stability (Chapter 7), foundation bearing capacity, and lateral earth pressures, where shear strength may be fully mobilized.",
      "key_points": [
        "Elastic solutions are appropriate when shear stress is significantly below shear strength; most Chapter 4 methods assume elastic conditions.",
        "If shear stress < ~1/3 of ultimate shear strength, elastic theory gives approximately correct stresses (Davis and Selvadurai 1996).",
        "Plastic solutions assume full mobilization of shear strength and apply to slope stability, bearing capacity, and lateral earth pressure problems.",
        "The choice between elastic and plastic analysis depends on how close the stress state is to failure."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when selecting the appropriate stress analysis framework (elastic vs. plastic) for a geotechnical problem."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2",
      "title": "Stress Conditions at a Point",
      "summary": "Parent section covering the full treatment of stress conditions at a point within a soil mass, including total vertical stress, pore water pressure, effective stress, horizontal stress, and the influence of applied loads. All subsections detail the components and equations needed for a complete stress state analysis.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever an engineer needs to determine the complete stress state at a specific point within a soil or rock mass."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1",
      "title": "Stress Conditions in Soil",
      "summary": "Introduces the conceptual framework for stress in soil: soil is idealized as a continuum despite being a particulate medium; stress is force per unit area. Covers normal and shear stress components on a plane, and notes that total normal stress is divided into effective normal stress and pore pressure.",
      "body": "Soil consists of a compilation of discrete particles, water, and air in varying proportions. Similarly, rock may contain a combination of the mineral components and any void space that may be filled with water or air. These discrete systems are idealized in stress analysis by assuming the soil acts as continuous solid mass without holes or gaps. In this continuum manner, stress is simply conceived as force per unit area and the contact forces at the soil particle level are not considered.\n\nStress in soil is the result of forces from the self-weight of the overlying and surrounding soil plus any external loading, such as structures or ponded water. For a given plane, it can be particularly useful to consider stress in terms of its normal and shear components. The normal stress can be defined as the sum of the forces acting perpendicular to a plane divided by the area of that plane. Similarly shear stress in a particular direction is ratio of the force acting tangent to a plane divided by its area.\n\nThe total normal stress on any plane within the ground is based on the sum of all forces acting on the plane in question. The total stress may be divided into two parts: the effective normal stress and the pore pressure.",
      "key_points": [
        "Soil is a particulate medium but is idealized as a continuum for stress analysis; contact forces at particle level are not considered.",
        "Stress = force per unit area; normal stress acts perpendicular to a plane, shear stress acts tangent to a plane.",
        "Total normal stress = effective normal stress + pore water pressure.",
        "Stress sources include self-weight of overlying soil and external loads such as structures or ponded water."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies as foundational background when setting up any stress analysis within a soil or rock mass."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1.1",
      "title": "Total Vertical Stress",
      "summary": "Defines total vertical stress (overburden stress) as the normal stress on a horizontal plane at depth, calculated by summing the product of thickness and total unit weight for all overlying layers (Eq. 4-1). Emphasizes inclusion of ponded water as an additional layer. Illustrated by Figure 4-1.",
      "body": "The total vertical stress (or overburden stress) is the normal stress acting on a horizontal plane at some depth within the soil. The total vertical stress, σv, at a particular depth is calculated by multiplying the thickness (zi) of all overlying materials by the total unit weight (γt,i) of each material:\n\nσv = Σ(zi · γt,i)   (Eq. 4-1)\n\nIt is imperative to include the weight of water resting on the ground surface (i.e., ponded water) in calculations of total vertical stress. Ponded water can be considered by adding a layer to the total stress calculations with thickness equal to the water depth and unit weight equal to the unit weight of water.\n\nThe calculation of total vertical stress is illustrated in Figure 4-1.",
      "key_points": [
        "Total vertical stress σv = Σ(zi · γt,i), summing thickness × total unit weight for each overlying layer (Eq. 4-1).",
        "Ponded water must be included as an additional layer with thickness = water depth and unit weight = γw.",
        "Figure 4-1 illustrates the calculation of vertical stresses under hydrostatic conditions."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-1",
          "description": "Total vertical stress at depth: σv = sum of (zi × γt,i) for all n overlying layers, where zi is layer thickness and γt,i is total unit weight of layer i.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.total_vertical_stress"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-1: Calculation of Vertical Stresses for Hydrostatic Conditions"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when calculating overburden or total vertical stress at any depth in a multi-layer soil profile, including profiles with ponded water."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1.2",
      "title": "Pore Water Pressure",
      "summary": "Explains pore water pressure (u) as derived from pressure head hp via Eq. 4-2 (u = hp·γw). Distinguishes hydrostatic conditions (static water, pressure head = depth below water table) from flowing water conditions requiring flow net or seepage analysis. Notes pore pressure acts equally in all directions and is sometimes called neutral stress.",
      "body": "The energy present in ponded water or groundwater is often expressed in terms of the total hydraulic head, which has pressure, elevation, and velocity components. The velocity component is typically ignored in most geotechnical applications. The pore water pressure (u) can be found from the pressure head (hp) as\n\nu = hp · γw   (Eq. 4-2)\n\nwhere: γw = the unit weight of water (62.4 pcf or 9.81 kN/m³).\n\nWhen water is static (not flowing), the total head is constant throughout the system, and elevation head converts directly to pressure head. This is referred to as a hydrostatic condition, and the pressure head is simply equal to the distance below the groundwater table or phreatic surface.\n\nFlowing water loses energy as it flows through the soil and the total head decreases in the direction of flow. For flowing water conditions, a flow net or some other type of seepage analysis must be performed. Pore water pressure at any point can be determined by first calculating the total head and the elevation head at any point in the ground. The pressure head for flowing water is found by subtracting the elevation head from the total head. Water pressures act in all directions equally because the water does not sustain shear stress. For this reason, orientation of the pore water pressure is not important. In some older publications, pore water pressure is also called neutral stress.",
      "key_points": [
        "Pore water pressure u = hp · γw (Eq. 4-2), where hp is the pressure head and γw = 62.4 pcf (9.81 kN/m³).",
        "Under hydrostatic conditions, pressure head equals depth below the groundwater table or phreatic surface.",
        "For flowing water, a flow net or seepage analysis is required; pressure head = total head − elevation head.",
        "Pore water pressure acts equally in all directions (water cannot sustain shear stress); sometimes called neutral stress."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-2",
          "description": "Pore water pressure from pressure head: u = hp × γw, where hp is pressure head and γw is unit weight of water (62.4 pcf or 9.81 kN/m³).",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.pore_water_pressure"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when determining pore water pressure at a point under either static (hydrostatic) or seepage (flowing water) groundwater conditions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1.3",
      "title": "Effective Vertical Stress",
      "summary": "Defines effective vertical stress σ'v as total vertical stress minus pore water pressure at the same point (Eq. 4-3). This is the stress carried by the soil skeleton and governs soil strength and compressibility behavior.",
      "body": "The effective vertical stress (σʹv) is found by subtracting the pore water pressure at any point from the total vertical stress at the same point\n\nσʹv = σv − u   (Eq. 4-3).",
      "key_points": [
        "Effective vertical stress σ'v = σv − u (Eq. 4-3); represents the stress carried by the soil skeleton.",
        "Pore water pressure must be determined at the same point and depth as the total vertical stress.",
        "Effective stress governs soil strength, compressibility, and consolidation behavior."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-3",
          "description": "Effective vertical stress: σ'v = σv − u, where σv is total vertical stress and u is pore water pressure at the same point.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.effective_vertical_stress"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever the engineer needs the effective vertical stress at a point, which is required for strength, compressibility, and consolidation analyses."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1.4",
      "title": "Horizontal Stress",
      "summary": "Covers calculation of horizontal stress in soil. Effective horizontal stress σ'h is calculated as K·σ'v (Eq. 4-4), where K is the lateral earth pressure coefficient depending on stress history and confinement. Total horizontal stress adds pore pressure back (Eq. 4-5). Table 4-1 summarizes K types (K0, KA, KP) and their applications.",
      "body": "Horizontal stress in a soil mass is influenced by the effective vertical stress, the geologic stress history, and lateral confinement conditions. Horizontal stress cannot be calculated directly from the soil profile and is typically calculated as a proportion of the effective vertical stress:\n\nσʹh = K · σʹv   (Eq. 4-4).\n\nThe lateral earth pressure coefficient (K) depends on stress history and lateral confinement conditions. Common types, applications, and sources of lateral earth pressure coefficients are summarized in Table 4-1.\n\nThe total horizontal stress can be found by adding the pore water pressure at any point onto the effective horizontal stress\n\nσh = σʹh + u   (Eq. 4-5).",
      "key_points": [
        "Effective horizontal stress σ'h = K · σ'v (Eq. 4-4); K depends on stress history and lateral confinement.",
        "Three key lateral earth pressure coefficients: at-rest K0, active KA, and passive KP — see Table 4-1 for applications and methods.",
        "Total horizontal stress σh = σ'h + u (Eq. 4-5); pore pressure is added to effective horizontal stress.",
        "Horizontal stress cannot be read directly from the soil profile; it must be estimated or measured.",
        "K0 applies to level natural ground and unyielding walls; KA to yielding walls; KP to wall toe resistance."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-4",
          "description": "Effective horizontal stress: σ'h = K × σ'v, where K is the lateral earth pressure coefficient and σ'v is effective vertical stress.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.effective_horizontal_stress"
        },
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-5",
          "description": "Total horizontal stress: σh = σ'h + u, where σ'h is effective horizontal stress and u is pore water pressure.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.total_horizontal_stress"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-1: Lateral Earth Pressure Coefficients"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when computing horizontal stresses at a point in a soil mass for retaining wall design, slope stability, or lateral earth pressure analysis."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-2.1.5",
      "title": "Applied Loads",
      "summary": "Discusses how external (non-soil) applied loads create changes in total stress within the soil mass, affecting pore pressures and causing settlement. Defines critical depth (10% of σ'v for fine-grained, 20% for coarse-grained soils). Distinguishes flexible vs. rigid foundation load distributions, referencing Figure 4-2 and pointing to Sections 4-3 and 4-6 for analytical and numerical methods.",
      "body": "Many civil engineering applications must consider the effects of external (non-soil) loads applied at the surface or at some depth within the soil mass. The influence of existing loads must be included in total stress calculations. New loads cause changes in total stress within the soil. These load changes will cause changes in the pore water pressures as discussed in more detail in Chapter 5. The duration of the changes in pore water pressure will depend on the permeability and compressibility of the soil.\n\nAnalytical methods for calculating changes in stress are provided in Section 4-3, including methods for point loads, line loads, and uniformly loaded areas. In Section 4-6, numerical methods for calculating changes in stress are summarized.\n\nChanges in total stress caused by applied loads should be within the soil mass to at least the critical depth. The critical depth is the depth over which soil compression caused by the changes in stress contributes to significant surface settlement. The critical depth in fine-grained soils corresponds to the depth at which the change in stress is less than 10% of the existing vertical effective stress. In coarse-grained soils, the critical depth occurs when the change in stress is less than 20% of the existing vertical effective stress.\n\nInteractions between the applied load and the soil foundation must be considered, especially for changes in stress very close to the load. The flexibility of the structure that applies a distributed load to the soil affects the distribution of the change in stress. A completely flexible load, such as a soil fill, will apply a uniform stress to the soil because the load can deform in proportion to the soil. The elastic solutions presented in Section 4-3 assume that the load is completely flexible.\n\nA foundation that is completely rigid with respect to the soil must undergo a uniform deformation. When a rigid foundation deforms uniformly into an elastic solid (i.e., undrained conditions for clay), the load must shift to the edges of the foundation, resulting in a pressure distribution that increases toward the edge (see Figure 4-2). In contrast, a rigid foundation on sand will cause yielding near the edges, resulting in a pressure distribution that decreases toward the edge.",
      "key_points": [
        "External loads create changes in total stress that affect pore water pressures; duration of pore pressure change depends on soil permeability and compressibility (see Chapter 5).",
        "Stress changes must be tracked to the critical depth: <10% of σ'v for fine-grained soils, <20% of σ'v for coarse-grained soils.",
        "Analytical methods for stress changes (point, line, and area loads) are in Section 4-3; numerical methods in Section 4-6.",
        "Flexible loads (e.g., soil fill) apply uniform stress; elastic solutions in Section 4-3 assume completely flexible loading.",
        "Rigid foundations on clay shift load to edges (pressure increases toward edge); rigid foundations on sand cause edge yielding (pressure decreases toward edge) — see Figure 4-2."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-2: Variation in Contact Pressure – a) Rigid Foundation and b) Completely Flexible Foundation"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when accounting for the effects of surface or embedded structural loads on subsurface stress distribution, settlement analysis, or foundation design."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3",
      "title": "Elastic Solutions for Stresses Due to Applied Loads",
      "summary": "Container section covering elastic solutions for computing stresses caused by applied surface loads. Includes semi-infinite Boussinesq solutions, chart-based methods, superposition, St. Venant's principle, and corrections for layered or anisotropic foundations. Primarily useful for consolidation settlement analyses and stress distribution understanding.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [
        "Covers Boussinesq and Westergaard elastic solutions for vertical stress changes under various load geometries.",
        "Methods are applicable to consolidation settlement analyses and for checking numerical models.",
        "Includes chart solutions, superposition, St. Venant's principle, and layered/anisotropic foundation corrections."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever elastic stress distribution beneath applied surface loads needs to be estimated for settlement or numerical model verification."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.1",
      "title": "Use and Applicability",
      "summary": "Describes when elastic stress solutions are appropriate. These methods are suited for simple stress-change analyses (especially consolidation settlement) and for understanding stress distribution principles. They also serve as checks on more complex numerical analyses but are not appropriate where soil is approaching plastic failure.",
      "body": "The elastic solutions presented in this section are useful for simple analyses of changes in stress, especially consolidation settlement. These methods are also useful for understanding the principles of stress distribution and for checking more complicated numerical analyses.",
      "key_points": [
        "Elastic solutions are best suited for consolidation settlement analyses and stress distribution understanding.",
        "Useful for verifying results of complex numerical analyses.",
        "Not appropriate where soil approaches plastic equilibrium; stability analysis methods should be used instead."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when selecting the appropriate method for stress change calculations, particularly for consolidation settlement or numerical model verification."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2",
      "title": "Semi-Infinite Elastic Conditions",
      "summary": "Container section covering the assumptions, stress distribution formulas, chart solutions, superposition, and St. Venant's principle for Boussinesq-based elastic stress solutions applied to homogeneous, semi-infinite, isotropic soil foundations.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [
        "Boussinesq solutions apply to homogeneous, isotropic, semi-infinite elastic media.",
        "Includes both formula-based and chart-based approaches for various load geometries.",
        "Superposition and St. Venant's principle extend the utility of these solutions."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when computing elastic stress changes in soil that can reasonably be assumed homogeneous, isotropic, and semi-infinite."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2.1",
      "title": "Assumptions",
      "summary": "Details the key assumptions underlying Boussinesq and related elastic solutions: homogeneous, elastic, continuous soil with loads applied at the surface. Plane strain applies for L/B ≥ 5. Rigid foundations violate uniform stress assumptions. Boussinesq solutions are not suited for shear stress analysis near plastic failure conditions.",
      "body": "The Boussinesq and related solutions assume the soil is a homogeneous, elastic material in which continuity is maintained and static equilibrium is satisfied. The applied load is completely flexible and is applied at the surface of the material. For embankment loading, the load is strictly vertical and no shear stress is applied to the foundation by the embankment. As discussed in Section 4-2.1.5, the stress distribution below a rigid foundation is not uniform and may not conform to the assumptions of the Boussinesq solutions.\n\nLoads with a length to width ratio (L/B) of at least five are commonly assumed to result in plane strain conditions, at least near the middle of the length. Under plane strain, deformation only occurs perpendicular to the long axis of the load and the changes in stress do not depend on the elastic properties of the material.\n\nThe Boussinesq solutions are not typically applicable to the calculation of shear stress for conditions where shear stress is becoming critical. In this case, the soil is approaching a state of plastic equilibrium and the assumption of elasticity no longer applies. In such cases, stability analysis methods, such as those in Chapter 7 and in DM 7.2, should be used.",
      "key_points": [
        "Soil is assumed homogeneous, elastic, continuous, and semi-infinite; loads are flexible and applied at the surface.",
        "Embankment loads are assumed strictly vertical with no shear applied to the foundation.",
        "L/B ≥ 5 justifies plane strain assumption; under plane strain, stress changes are independent of elastic properties.",
        "Rigid foundations produce non-uniform stress distributions that do not conform to Boussinesq assumptions.",
        "Boussinesq solutions are not valid for shear stress analysis near plastic failure; use stability analysis methods instead."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies before using any Boussinesq-based elastic stress solution to verify that the site and loading conditions satisfy the underlying assumptions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2.2",
      "title": "Stress Distribution Formulas",
      "summary": "Presents closed-form formulas (Table 4-2) for calculating vertical stress changes beneath point loads, uniform line loads, strip loads, uniformly loaded rectangular and circular areas, triangular loads, slope loads, terrace loads, and semi-infinite uniform loads. Also addresses horizontal and shear stresses requiring Poisson's ratio, commonly assumed at ν = 0.5.",
      "body": "Formulas for homogeneous, semi-infinite, isotropic foundations are summarized in Table 4-2. These formulas can be used for hand calculations for simple computations and to check the results of more complex numerical analyses. Such formulas can also easily be programmed into a spreadsheet solution. Additional formulas for other geometric and loading conditions are summarized in Poulos and Davis (1974).\n\nHorizontal and shear stresses caused by applied loads can also be determined from elastic solutions. In many cases, these calculations require a value of Poisson's ratio (ν) for the soil. Many of the common figures assume ν = 0.5 making it important to verify the value of ν that was used. One application of horizontal stress calculations is for the loading of unyielding walls as discussed in DM 7.2. For conditions where elastic solutions are suitable, the calculation of shear stress is typically not required.",
      "key_points": [
        "Table 4-2 provides vertical stress formulas for eight load geometries: point, line, strip, rectangular, circular, triangular, slope, terrace, and semi-infinite uniform loads.",
        "Formulas are suitable for hand calculations, spreadsheet implementation, and numerical model verification.",
        "Horizontal and shear stress calculations often require Poisson's ratio; many standard charts assume ν = 0.5.",
        "For unyielding wall loading, horizontal stress from elastic solutions is discussed in DM 7.2.",
        "Shear stress calculation is generally not required when elastic solutions are applicable."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-4",
        "Figure 4-5",
        "Figure 4-6"
      ],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-2"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when computing vertical (or horizontal) stress changes beneath specific standard load geometries using closed-form elastic formulas."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2.3",
      "title": "Chart Solutions for Vertical Stress beneath Regular Loads",
      "summary": "Describes chart-based solutions (Figures 4-4 through 4-7) for computing vertical stress changes beneath strip, square, rectangular, circular, and embankment loads using Boussinesq theory. All charts assume ν = 0.5. Example calculations are provided in Figure 4-9. Newmark/Jimenez Salas charts handled irregular areas historically; numerical analysis now preferred.",
      "body": "Figure 4-4 to Figure 4-7 provide chart solutions for regularly shaped loads and Boussinesq theory. These charts are all based on the assumption of ν = 0.5, where required, to determine the change in vertical stress. Example calculations are provided in Figure 4-9. Additional guidance on the calculation of changes in stress under elastic conditions can be found in Poulos and Davis (1974).\n\nIn the past, changes in stress caused by irregularly shaped loaded areas were calculated using chart solutions such as those proposed by Newmark (1942) and Jimenez Salas (1948). However, for complex loading conditions, numerical analysis has become the most common means to evaluate changes in stress.",
      "key_points": [
        "Figures 4-4 through 4-7 provide Boussinesq chart solutions for strip, square, rectangular, circular, and embankment loads.",
        "All charts assume ν = 0.5; verify this assumption before use.",
        "Figure 4-9 provides worked examples demonstrating use of the stress distribution charts.",
        "Newmark (1942) and Jimenez Salas (1948) charts previously used for irregular areas; numerical analysis is now preferred for complex geometries."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-4",
        "Figure 4-5",
        "Figure 4-6",
        "Figure 4-7",
        "Figure 4-9"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when using graphical influence factor charts to determine vertical stress changes beneath regularly shaped surface loads under Boussinesq assumptions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2.4",
      "title": "Superposition",
      "summary": "Explains how linear elastic assumptions underlying Boussinesq solutions permit superposition of stresses from multiple loads. Stresses from individual loads can be added or subtracted at a common point, enabling computation of stress changes below and outside loaded areas for complex geometries, as illustrated in Figure 4-8.",
      "body": "The assumption of linear elasticity inherent in the Boussinesq solutions allows for superposition of stresses that result from applied loads. This means that the change in stress from one load can be added or subtracted from those caused by other loads, provided the same point is being considered in all cases. This principle is especially useful for determining the change in stress below and outside of loaded areas as illustrated in Figure 4-8.",
      "key_points": [
        "Linear elastic assumption permits superposition: stresses from multiple loads can be added or subtracted at the same point.",
        "Superposition is especially useful for evaluating stress changes outside or beneath complex loaded area geometries.",
        "Figure 4-8 illustrates application of superposition for determining vertical stress changes."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-8"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when combining stress contributions from multiple loads or computing stress at points outside simple loaded area boundaries using Boussinesq solutions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.2.5",
      "title": "St. Venant's Principle",
      "summary": "Describes St. Venant's principle as applied to stress distribution: at sufficient depth, stress changes from statically equivalent loads become equal regardless of load shape. Practically, this allows point load or line load approximations for compact or strip loads. Commonly applied for depths greater than three times the loaded area width.",
      "body": "St. Venant's principle is another useful concept for the calculation of change in stress due to applied loads. According to this principle, the change in stress caused by two statically equivalent loads becomes equal as the distance from the load becomes sufficiently large. Practically, this means that a square or circular load can be replaced with a point load, or a strip load can be considered a line load. It is commonly assumed that St. Venant's principle can be applied to the calculation of change in vertical stress, Δσz, for depths greater than three times the width of the applied load.",
      "key_points": [
        "St. Venant's principle: stresses from statically equivalent loads converge at sufficient distance from the load.",
        "Square or circular loads may be approximated as point loads; strip loads may be treated as line loads at depth.",
        "Rule of thumb: St. Venant's principle applies for depths > 3× the load width when computing Δσz."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when simplifying stress calculations at depth by substituting point or line load approximations for distributed loads using the 3× width depth threshold."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.3",
      "title": "Layered or Anisotropic Foundations",
      "summary": "Addresses limitations of Boussinesq solutions for real soils that are neither homogeneous nor isotropic. Different layers have varying elastic moduli and horizontal stiffness often exceeds vertical stiffness. Introduces Westergaard-type analysis and layered foundation methods as alternatives, with numerical analysis preferred for complex profiles.",
      "body": "While the Boussinesq solutions offer a relatively simple means to calculate changes in stress, soil is not a homogeneous, isotropic, and semi-infinite medium. For example, different layers typically have different values of elastic modulus. Soil layers are often more rigid horizontally than vertically. These deviations from the assumptions of Boussinesq have led to the development of other methods for the calculation of changes in stress, most notably the Westergaard type of analysis.",
      "key_points": [
        "Real soils deviate from Boussinesq assumptions: layers have different elastic moduli and horizontal stiffness often exceeds vertical stiffness.",
        "These deviations necessitate alternative methods such as Westergaard analysis or layered foundation solutions.",
        "Numerical analysis is the preferred method for complex, highly variable soil profiles."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when the soil profile has significant layering or anisotropy that violates the homogeneous isotropic assumptions of Boussinesq solutions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.3.1",
      "title": "Westergaard Analysis",
      "summary": "Describes Westergaard analysis, which models soil reinforced by closely spaced horizontal layers preventing horizontal displacement. Predicts lower stress changes than Boussinesq. Best suited for alternating stiff/soft soil profiles (e.g., soft clay with horizontal sand layers). Figure 4-10 provides rectangular area influence factors using Westergaard theory.",
      "body": "Westergaard analysis assumes that the soil below the load is reinforced by closely spaced horizontal layers that prevent horizontal displacement. This reinforcement effect causes the changes in stress predicted by Westergaard to be less than those calculated by the Boussinesq assumptions. The Westergaard type of analysis is most appropriate for soil profiles that have alternating layers of stiff and soft soils, such as soft clay with intermittent horizontal layers of sand. Figure 4-10 provides influence factors for points below the corner of a rectangular loaded area, assuming Westergaard theory.",
      "key_points": [
        "Westergaard assumes closely spaced horizontal reinforcing layers that prevent horizontal displacement.",
        "Westergaard predicts smaller stress changes than Boussinesq.",
        "Most appropriate for alternating stiff/soft soil profiles (e.g., soft clay interbedded with sand layers).",
        "Figure 4-10 provides Westergaard influence factors for the corner of a rectangular loaded area."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-10"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when the foundation soil consists of alternating stiff and soft horizontal layers, making Westergaard a more appropriate model than Boussinesq."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-3.3.2",
      "title": "Layered Foundations",
      "summary": "Addresses soil profiles with thick layers of significantly different elastic properties, where Boussinesq predictions diverge substantially from actual stress changes. Analytical and chart-based solutions using rigidity factors (e.g., Mehta and Veletsos 1959) have been proposed, but numerical analysis is the preferred approach for these conditions.",
      "body": "Soil profiles may have layers of significant thickness and very different elastic properties. The changes in vertical stress induced in these cases differs significantly from that predicted by Boussinesq assumptions. Analytical and chart-based solutions have been suggested to account for such differences using rigidity factors (e.g., Mehta and Veletsos 1959). For these conditions, numerical analysis is the preferred method to determine changes in stress or as a means of comparison to simpler solutions.",
      "key_points": [
        "Thick layers with very different elastic properties produce stress changes that deviate significantly from Boussinesq predictions.",
        "Rigidity factor approaches (e.g., Mehta and Veletsos 1959) provide analytical/chart corrections for layered profiles.",
        "Numerical analysis is the preferred method for layered foundation stress calculations."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when the foundation profile contains thick layers with significantly different elastic moduli, requiring corrections or numerical analysis beyond basic Boussinesq solutions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4",
      "title": "Shallow Pipes and Conduits",
      "summary": "Introduces the analysis of stresses on shallow pipes and conduits due to applied loads. Identifies key influencing factors (pipe rigidity, depth of cover, load type, span width, construction method, and pipe shape) and directs engineers to empirical procedures, numerical methods, and references for more detailed analysis.",
      "body": "The stresses on shallow pipes and conduits due to applied loads is one important application of concepts presented in this chapter. The factors influencing these stresses include the relative rigidity of the pipe to the soil, the depth of cover, the type of loading, the maximum width (span) of the structure, the method of construction, and the shape of the pipe.\n\nThis section presents simple empirical procedures based on observations. More detailed analysis can be conducted numerically or by consulting Moser (1990) or American Lifelines Alliance (2001).",
      "key_points": [
        "Stresses on buried pipes depend on pipe rigidity, cover depth, load type, span, construction method, and pipe shape.",
        "Empirical procedures in this section are based on field observations and are simplified approaches.",
        "More detailed analysis can be performed numerically or by reference to Moser (1990) or American Lifelines Alliance (2001)."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever an engineer needs to assess loads and stresses on shallow buried pipes and conduits."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.1",
      "title": "General",
      "summary": "Provides an overview of the factors governing stresses in shallow pipes and conduits, including pipe rigidity relative to soil, depth of cover, load type, span width, construction method, and pipe shape. Directs engineers to empirical procedures and references for more detailed analysis.",
      "body": "The stresses on shallow pipes and conduits due to applied loads is one important application of concepts presented in this chapter. The factors influencing these stresses include the relative rigidity of the pipe to the soil, the depth of cover, the type of loading, the maximum width (span) of the structure, the method of construction, and the shape of the pipe.\n\nThis section presents simple empirical procedures based on observations. More detailed analysis can be conducted numerically or by consulting Moser (1990) or American Lifelines Alliance (2001).",
      "key_points": [
        "Six primary factors influence stresses on buried pipes: relative rigidity, cover depth, load type, span, construction method, and shape.",
        "Simple empirical procedures based on observations are presented in this section.",
        "For more complex cases, numerical methods or references such as Moser (1990) and American Lifelines Alliance (2001) should be consulted."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies as an introductory overview when beginning analysis of shallow pipe and conduit stresses."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.2",
      "title": "Vertical Loads on Rigid Pipe",
      "summary": "Covers the calculation of vertical dead and live loads on rigid pipes (precast concrete, cast-in-place concrete, and cast iron) in trenches, using Marston-Anderson-Spangler methods for dead load and Boussinesq-based stress equations with impact factors for live loads.",
      "body": "Rigid pipes are those made of precast concrete, cast-in-place concrete, or cast iron.",
      "key_points": [
        "Rigid pipes include precast concrete, cast-in-place concrete, and cast iron pipe types.",
        "Dead loads are calculated using the Marston-Anderson-Spangler empirical approach.",
        "Live loads use Boussinesq theory multiplied by impact factors to account for dynamic effects."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when calculating vertical loads on rigid buried pipes made of concrete or cast iron."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.2.1",
      "title": "Dead Load",
      "summary": "Presents the Marston-Anderson (1913) and Spangler (1948) method for calculating dead load per unit length on a rigid pipe in a trench. Defines the load coefficient Cd as a function of trench geometry, lateral earth pressure, and friction, with tabulated soil parameters from Moser (1990).",
      "body": "Estimates of the load caused by vertical stress on a rigid pipe can be made using the approach suggested by Marston and Anderson (1913) and subsequent work by Spangler (1948). The load per length of pipe (Wd) in a trench can be calculated as:\n\nWd = Cd × γt × Bd²   (Eq. 4-6)\n\nwhere:\nCd = a load coefficient,\nγt = the total unit weight of the soil, and\nBd = the width of the trench.\n\nThe value of Cd can be calculated as:\n\nCd = (1 - e^(-2K µ' H/Bd)) / (2Kµ')   (Eq. 4-7)\n\nwhere:\nH = the depth of the trench above the top of the pipe,\nK = a lateral earth pressure coefficient, and\nµ' = the coefficient of friction for the trench fill.\n\nRecommended values of γt, K, and µ' for use with this equation are provided in Table 4-3 as summarized by Moser (1990).",
      "key_points": [
        "Dead load per unit length on a rigid pipe in a trench is Wd = Cd × γt × Bd² (Eq. 4-6).",
        "The load coefficient Cd (Eq. 4-7) depends on trench depth H, trench width Bd, lateral earth pressure coefficient K, and friction coefficient µ'.",
        "Recommended values of γt, K, and µ' for six soil types are given in Table 4-3 (after Moser 1990).",
        "The product Kµ' ranges from 0.11 (saturated clay) to 0.17 (topsoil, dry sand, saturated sand)."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-6",
          "description": "Dead load per unit length of rigid pipe in a trench: Wd = Cd × γt × Bd²",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.rigid_pipe_trench_load"
        },
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-7",
          "description": "Load coefficient Cd for rigid pipe trench: Cd = (1 - e^(-2Kµ'H/Bd)) / (2Kµ'), where H is trench depth, Bd is trench width, K is lateral earth pressure coefficient, and µ' is friction coefficient",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.flexible_pipe_trench_load"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-3"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating the dead load on a rigid pipe buried in a trench using empirical methods."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.2.2",
      "title": "Live Load",
      "summary": "Describes how to calculate live loads on rigid buried pipes from vehicle traffic (trucks, railroad, airplanes) using Boussinesq theory with dynamic impact factors. References Table 4-2 for vertical stress equations, Table 4-4 for impact factors by depth and vehicle type, and Table 4-5 for combined live load pressures, noting stress becomes negligible at 8, 30, and 24 ft depth for truck, rail, and airport loads respectively.",
      "body": "The primary live loads considered in the design of buried pipes are those from vehicles, including trucks, railroad, and airplanes. The stress at the top of the pipe is typically calculated using Boussinesq theory multiplied by an impact factor that accounts for dynamic effects. The equations presented in Table 4-2 can be used to estimate the vertical stress transferred to the top of the pipe from surface loading. Appropriate live load impact factors are summarized in Table 4-4.\n\nLive load pressures (including an impact factor of 1.5) are summarized in Table 4-5. The values in this table indicate that the changes in stress become negligible below depths of 8 feet, 30 feet, and 24 feet for standard truck, railroad, and airport loads, respectively.",
      "key_points": [
        "Live loads from trucks, railroad, and airplanes are the primary vehicle loads considered for buried pipe design.",
        "Boussinesq theory is used to calculate stress at the pipe top, multiplied by impact factors for dynamic effects.",
        "Impact factors range from 1.00 to 1.75 depending on depth of cover and vehicle type (Table 4-4).",
        "Stress changes become negligible below 8 ft (truck), 30 ft (railroad), and 24 ft (airport) cover depths.",
        "Table 4-5 tabulates live load pressures including a 1.5 impact factor for standard truck, railway (Cooper E-80), and airport (180 kip gear) loading."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-2",
        "Table 4-4",
        "Table 4-5"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when calculating live loads from vehicle traffic on rigid buried pipes at varying depths of cover."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.3",
      "title": "Vertical Loads on Flexible Pipe",
      "summary": "Describes behavior and load calculation methods for flexible pipes (corrugated metal, plastic, thin-wall smooth steel). Unlike rigid pipes, flexible pipes deform under load and develop horizontal restraining pressures. Vertical pressure depends on surrounding soil conditions, and arching effects can increase or decrease overburden pressure significantly.",
      "body": "Flexible pipes include corrugated metal, plastic, and thin-wall smooth steel pipes. These pipes deform when loaded and develop horizontal restraining pressures on the sides that may be approximately equal to the vertical pressure if the backfill is well-compacted. The vertical pressure on the top of the pipe depends on the surrounding soil. In highly compressible soil, the vertical pressure may exceed the overburden pressure. In contrast, arching in coarse-grained soils may significantly reduce the overburden pressure.",
      "key_points": [
        "Flexible pipes include corrugated metal, plastic, and thin-wall smooth steel.",
        "Deformation under load generates horizontal restraining pressures on the pipe sides, potentially equal to vertical pressure with well-compacted backfill.",
        "In highly compressible soils, vertical pressure on the pipe can exceed overburden pressure.",
        "Arching in coarse-grained soils can significantly reduce the effective overburden pressure on flexible pipes."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when analyzing vertical loads on flexible buried pipes such as corrugated metal, plastic, or thin-wall steel."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.3.1",
      "title": "Dead Load",
      "summary": "Presents two methods for estimating dead load on flexible pipe: the Marston load theory (Eq. 4-8), which uses the rigid pipe load coefficient Cd but may be unconservative due to equal pipe/soil stiffness assumption, and the more conservative prism load method (Eq. 4-9), which uses the full weight of soil directly above the pipe.",
      "body": "For very flexible pipe with outside diameter (Bc) the Marston load theory predicts a load (Wc) of:\n\nWc = Cd × γt × Bc × D   (Eq. 4-8)\n\nwhere:\nCd = the load coefficient for rigid pipe (see Equation 4-7),\nγt = the total unit weight of the trench backfill, and\nD = the outer diameter of the pipe (Moser 1990).\n\nThis method assumes that the pipe stiffness and soil stiffness are equal, which may lead to unconservative values of Wc.\n\nThe prism load is a more conservative approach for determining the dead load on a buried flexible pipe. The prism load (Wp) is simply the total weight of the soil above the pipe and is equal to:\n\nWp = γt × H × D   (Eq. 4-9)\n\nwhere:\nγt = the backfill total unit weight,\nH = the depth of soil cover, and\nD = the outer diameter of the pipe.",
      "key_points": [
        "Marston load theory for flexible pipe (Eq. 4-8): Wc = Cd × γt × Bc × D, using the same Cd as for rigid pipe.",
        "Marston method assumes equal pipe and soil stiffness, which may produce unconservative (too low) dead load estimates.",
        "Prism load method (Eq. 4-9): Wp = γt × H × D — simply the full overburden weight of soil above the pipe.",
        "The prism load is the more conservative (recommended) approach for flexible pipe dead load design."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-8",
          "description": "Marston dead load on very flexible pipe: Wc = Cd × γt × Bc × D, where Cd is the rigid pipe load coefficient (Eq. 4-7), γt is backfill unit weight, and D is outer pipe diameter",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.flexible_pipe_prism_load"
        },
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-9",
          "description": "Prism load (conservative dead load) on buried flexible pipe: Wp = γt × H × D, where γt is backfill unit weight, H is depth of soil cover, and D is outer pipe diameter",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.flexible_pipe_prism_load"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating the dead load on flexible buried pipes using either the Marston or prism load approach."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.3.2",
      "title": "Live Load",
      "summary": "Specifies that the same Boussinesq-based live load approach described in Section 4-4.2.2 for rigid pipes should be used to calculate live loads on flexible pipes. No separate method is provided for flexible pipe live load calculation.",
      "body": "The Boussinesq approach described in Section 4-4.2.2 should also be used to calculate live loads on flexible pipes.",
      "key_points": [
        "Live loads on flexible pipes are calculated using the same Boussinesq method as for rigid pipes (Section 4-4.2.2).",
        "No separate or modified live load method is required for flexible pipe types."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when calculating live loads from vehicle traffic on flexible buried pipes."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-4.4",
      "title": "Long Span Metal Culverts",
      "summary": "Explains that standard rigid and flexible pipe methods do not apply to long span metal culverts, which require finite element analysis (e.g., CANDE software). Distinguishes between shallow cover (less than one-fourth of culvert span, requiring flexural stress design) and deep cover (greater than one-fourth of span, requiring ring compression design) conditions with guidance from Duncan (1979).",
      "body": "The previously discussed methods do not apply to the calculation of stresses and design of long span metal culverts. The use of finite element analysis software specifically formulated for culverts, such as CANDE, is likely required. For additional guidance see Duncan (1979).\n\nFor the design of long span metal culverts, the engineer must distinguish between shallow and deep cover conditions (Duncan 1979). Shallow cover conditions apply to cases where the cover is less than one-fourth of the culvert span. Culverts with shallow cover must be designed for flexural stresses caused by live loads. The factor of safety is calculated by comparing the predicted axial stresses and moment loading to the culvert capacity.\n\nIn contrast, deep cover conditions occur when the cover is greater than one-fourth of the span. Deep cover culverts only require design for ring compression, such that the seams don't collapse under the design loads. Design axial ring loads are higher than those calculated solely based on ring compression theory. This occurs because the culvert is much stiffer in ring compression than the surrounding soil and a negative arching condition occurs.",
      "key_points": [
        "Standard pipe load methods do not apply to long span metal culverts; finite element software such as CANDE is required.",
        "Shallow cover: cover depth < one-fourth of culvert span; design must address flexural stresses from live loads.",
        "Deep cover: cover depth > one-fourth of span; design focuses on ring compression to prevent seam collapse.",
        "Negative arching occurs in deep cover culverts because the culvert is stiffer in ring compression than the surrounding soil, increasing design axial ring loads beyond simple ring compression theory values.",
        "Refer to Duncan (1979) for additional guidance on long span metal culvert design."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when designing long span metal culverts where standard pipe load methods are insufficient and cover depth classification (shallow vs. deep) governs the design approach."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5",
      "title": "Deep Underground Openings",
      "summary": "Container section covering the analysis of deep underground openings including tunnels and vertical shafts. Addresses pre-excavation equilibrium, stress redistribution, arching, soil-structure interaction, and the distinction between deep (cover/diameter > 3) and shallow (cover/diameter < 2) openings. Introduces numerical and simplified analytical methods for stress and deformation calculations.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [
        "Excavation disturbs pre-existing equilibrium, transferring load to surrounding rock, soil, or support system.",
        "Cover-to-diameter ratio < 2: shallow opening (ignore arching); ratio > 3: deep opening (arching beneficial).",
        "Deep openings benefit from arching; stresses depend heavily on deformation allowed and lining restraint.",
        "Numerical methods (e.g., FEA) can accurately model complexity; simplified methods useful for checking results.",
        "Time-dependent response is common, especially in saturated compressible soils."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever a deep underground opening (tunnel or shaft) is being analyzed for stress, deformation, or support design."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.1",
      "title": "General Factors",
      "summary": "Discusses the fundamental mechanics of deep underground openings: pre-excavation equilibrium, stress transfer upon excavation, time-dependent soil response, soil-structure interaction, and the criteria distinguishing deep from shallow openings. Establishes that cover-to-diameter ratio governs arching behavior and that tunneling method significantly affects loading on support.",
      "body": "Prior to excavation of a tunnel, the rock or soil is typically at a state of equilibrium under the stresses imposed by overburden and external loads. Excavation disturbs that equilibrium condition and requires load to transfer to surrounding rock, soil, or tunnel support system. The soil and rock will always exhibit an immediate response to the changes in stress caused by the excavation. Often a time-dependent response also occurs, especially in saturated compressible soils. A good discussion of the development of equilibrium conditions during and after tunneling in soft ground, as well as tunneling and support methods, is provided in FHWA (2009).\n\nChanges in stress are typically accompanied by displacement of the rock, soil, or support structure. Similar to retaining walls, some movement is desirable to create a suitable balance between the load carried by the structure and the load distributed to the soil. Due to the effects of this soil-structure interaction, the type of support system and tunneling method used will significantly affect the deformations experienced during and after tunneling, and therefore the loading imposed on tunnel support.\n\nThe stresses acting on an underground opening will also depend on the depth of the opening below the ground surface and the characteristics of the surrounding soil or rock. One common distinction between the terms deep and shallow compares the depth of cover to the diameter of the opening. Openings for which this ratio is less than 2 should be considered shallow and arching of the soil or rock should be ignored. Deep openings have a cover to diameter ratio greater than 3 and benefit from the effects of arching.\n\nFor deep underground openings, deformation toward the opening allows the release of stress and the development of arching in the surrounding soil or rock. For this reason, the stresses are heavily dependent on the amount of deformation allowed during construction and the degree of restraint provided by the lining.\n\nNumerical methods, such as finite element analysis, can be used to calculate stresses and deformations of underground openings. These methods can be quite accurate and account for significant complexity provided the soil and rock are characterized properly and the construction sequence is adequately modeled. The methods presented in this section are useful for simple calculations and to check the results of more complex numerical models.",
      "key_points": [
        "Pre-excavation equilibrium is disturbed by excavation, requiring load transfer to rock, soil, or support.",
        "Cover/diameter < 2: shallow (ignore arching); > 3: deep (arching provides benefit).",
        "Time-dependent response is especially important in saturated compressible soils.",
        "Tunneling method and support type significantly control deformations and load on support.",
        "FEA and other numerical methods are accurate if soil/rock is well-characterized and construction sequence modeled.",
        "Simplified methods in this section are useful for preliminary design and numerical model verification."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when assessing the general mechanics and classification of any deep underground opening prior to detailed analysis."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.2",
      "title": "Openings in Rock",
      "summary": "Container section that divides rock into two groups for tunnel stress analysis: (1) sound, non-swelling rock capable of sustaining tensile stress and (2) fractured, blocky, seamy, squeezing, or swelling rock. The primary distinction is the rock's ability to resist tensile stress and significant deformation. Subsections cover elastic analysis for intact rock and empirical rules for altered rock.",
      "body": "Rock can be separated into two groups for the purposes of determining stresses: (1) sound, non-swelling rock that can sustain considerable tensile stress and (2) fractured, blocky, seamy, squeezing, or swelling rock. For more detailed explanations of rock properties, see Chapter 1. The behavior of these two groups is distinguished primarily by the ability of the rock to resist tensile stress and/or significant deformation.",
      "key_points": [
        "Two rock groups for stress analysis: (1) sound non-swelling rock and (2) fractured/blocky/seamy/squeezing/swelling rock.",
        "Primary distinction is ability to resist tensile stress and significant deformation.",
        "Detailed rock property descriptions are provided in Chapter 1."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when classifying rock conditions surrounding a deep underground opening to select the appropriate stress analysis method."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.2.1",
      "title": "Sound Rock",
      "summary": "Covers elastic analysis for stress determination in intact, isotropic rock such as crystalline igneous, homogeneous sandstone, and limestone. Notes that spheroidal cavities produce lower surrounding stresses than tunnels of the same cross-section, and that elastic analysis can optimize pillar and opening arrangements by locating supports at stress concentrations.",
      "body": "Elastic analysis can be used to determine stresses surrounding tunnels or openings in intact, isotropic rock (e.g., crystalline igneous, homogeneous sandstone and limestone). Analytical methods are summarized in rock mechanics texts such as Goodman (1989).\n\nFor these materials, stresses in rock surrounding spheroidal cavities are lower than those for tunnels with the same cross-section. Elastic analysis can be used to determine the best arrangement of openings and pillars, such that supports are provided at locations of stress concentration.",
      "key_points": [
        "Elastic analysis is applicable for intact, isotropic rock (crystalline igneous, homogeneous sandstone, limestone).",
        "Spheroidal cavities produce lower surrounding stresses than tunnels of equivalent cross-section.",
        "Elastic analysis can optimize the arrangement of openings and pillars by identifying stress concentration locations.",
        "Reference: Goodman (1989) for analytical methods."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when analyzing stresses in tunnels or openings excavated in intact, isotropic sound rock."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.2.2",
      "title": "Broken and Fractured Rock",
      "summary": "Addresses pressure estimation for tunnels in chemically or mechanically altered rock using approximate empirical rules. Directs the user to Table 4-6 for rock load values and Table 4-7 for comparison of rock conditions with standard rock quality indices (RQD, Q, RMR), since elastic methods are not suitable for these materials.",
      "body": "Pressure on tunnels in chemically or mechanically altered rock must be analyzed by approximate rules based on experience, such as those presented in Table 4-6. The rock conditions used in Table 4-6 are compared to other common rock quality indices in Table 4-7.",
      "key_points": [
        "Elastic methods are not appropriate for broken/fractured rock; use empirical rules.",
        "Table 4-6 provides approximate rock load (Hp) based on rock condition category.",
        "Table 4-7 correlates rock conditions with RQD, Q index, and RMR for cross-referencing."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-6",
        "Table 4-7"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating tunnel support pressures in chemically or mechanically altered, broken, or fractured rock."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.2.3",
      "title": "Squeezing and Swelling Rock",
      "summary": "Describes the behavior of squeezing and swelling rock (categories 7–9 of Table 4-6/4-7), which originates from heavily preloaded clay deposits. These rocks contain clay minerals (ranging from non-swelling kaolinite to highly swelling montmorillonite) that expand upon stress relief, lose shear strength, and cause tunnel walls to squeeze inward.",
      "body": "Rocks in categories 7 to 9 of Table 4-6 and Table 4-7 are the result of clay deposits that have been heavily preloaded during their geologic history. The transition from very dense soil to soft rock is not well-defined. In some cases, very dense clays that have not fully lithified may be included in this category.\n\nRock properties are closely tied to the properties of the minerals from which it is comprised. The rocks in this category contain significant amounts of clay minerals with properties ranging from the non-swelling kaolinite group to the highly swelling montmorillonite group. Soil and rock that has a high fraction of clay minerals will tend to expand, absorb water, and lose shear strength while undergoing stress relief. Thus, rocks with significant amounts of clay minerals will tend to swell as a result of the stress relief around an underground opening. Swelling leads to a loss of shear strength and a tendency of the tunnel walls to squeeze into the opening.",
      "key_points": [
        "Squeezing/swelling rock (Table 4-6/4-7 categories 7–9) originates from heavily preloaded clay deposits.",
        "These rocks contain clay minerals ranging from non-swelling kaolinite to highly swelling montmorillonite.",
        "Stress relief causes clay-rich rocks to expand, absorb water, and lose shear strength.",
        "Swelling leads to loss of shear strength and tunnel wall squeezing into the opening.",
        "The boundary between very dense clay and soft rock is not well-defined; dense unlithified clays may be included."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-6",
        "Table 4-7"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when evaluating tunnels in squeezing or swelling rock conditions (rock quality categories 7–9) where clay mineral content drives volume change and strength loss."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.3",
      "title": "Loads on Underground Openings in Rock",
      "summary": "Container section addressing methods for estimating vertical and horizontal loads on underground openings in rock, including empirical rock load estimation using Terzaghi's classifications (Table 4-6), Coulomb wedge analysis for horizontal pressures, and alternative empirical methods based on Q and RMR systems.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [
        "Covers vertical rock load (Hp), horizontal pressures, and alternative empirical support pressure methods.",
        "Table 4-6 (Terzaghi 1946) is the primary empirical basis for vertical roof pressure estimation.",
        "Horizontal pressures use active wedge (Coulomb) analysis with rock shear strength parameters.",
        "Q index (Barton et al. 1974) and RMR (Bieniawski 1976/1990) are alternative empirical methods."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating vertical or horizontal loads imposed on tunnel support systems in rock."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.3.1",
      "title": "Vertical Rock Load",
      "summary": "Provides the empirical method for estimating vertical roof pressure on tunnel linings in rock using Table 4-6 (based on Terzaghi 1946). The design load height Hp is a function of tunnel width B and, for lower quality rock, also tunnel height Ht. Total vertical pressure equals Hp multiplied by the rock unit weight. Notes limitations: applies to tunnel widths of 16–32 ft and modern tunneling methods may differ.",
      "body": "A common starting point for the estimation of vertical roof pressure is found in Table 4-6. It should be noted that the values presented in this table were largely based on observations by Terzaghi (1946) for tunnel widths in the range of 16 to 32 feet (5 to 10 meters) prior to the advent of 'modern' tunneling methods. Table 4-6 provides an approximate means of calculating the vertical pressure or rock load that must be supported by roof lining. The height of rock (Hp) that must be supported is a function of the tunnel width (B) for high quality rock and also the tunnel height (Ht) for lower quality rock. The total vertical pressure can be found by multiplying Hp by the total unit weight of the rock.",
      "key_points": [
        "Table 4-6 provides approximate vertical rock load Hp as a function of rock condition category.",
        "For high-quality rock, Hp depends only on tunnel width B; for lower-quality rock, also on tunnel height Ht.",
        "Total vertical pressure = Hp × total unit weight of rock.",
        "Table 4-6 based on Terzaghi (1946) observations for tunnel widths of 16–32 ft; may not fully apply to modern tunneling.",
        "Applies to tunnels at depth greater than 1.5·(B + Ht); tunnel assumed below water table unless noted."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-6",
        "Table 4-7"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating vertical roof pressure for tunnel lining design in rock using Terzaghi-based empirical classification."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.3.2",
      "title": "Horizontal Pressures",
      "summary": "Describes how to estimate horizontal pressures on rock tunnels using an active wedge (Coulomb) analysis. The vertical forces include the weight of the active wedge and the rock above it at height Hp. Shear strength parameters are selected per Chapter 3. The critical wedge inclination is governed by rock shear strength or rock structure, and weak bedding planes must be considered.",
      "body": "Horizontal pressures acting on a tunnel can be approximated using an active wedge type analysis, such as Coulomb, with the diagram shown at the bottom of Table 4-6. The vertical forces included in these calculations are the weight of the active wedge and the weight of the rock for a height (Hp) above the wedge. Shear strength parameters can be assumed or selected using guidance in Chapter 3. The critical inclination of the active wedge may be determined by either the shear strength of the rock or by the rock structure. The possibility of movement along a weak bedding plane or stratum should be considered.",
      "key_points": [
        "Horizontal tunnel pressures in rock are estimated using Coulomb active wedge analysis.",
        "Vertical forces include the weight of the active wedge plus rock of height Hp above it.",
        "Shear strength parameters selected per Chapter 3 guidance.",
        "Critical wedge inclination governed by rock shear strength or rock structure (e.g., bedding planes).",
        "Movement along weak bedding planes or strata must be explicitly considered."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-6"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating horizontal earth pressures on the sides of rock tunnels using limit equilibrium methods."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.3.3",
      "title": "Other Methods for Tunnel Support Pressures",
      "summary": "Summarizes alternative empirical methods for estimating tunnel support pressures in rock beyond Terzaghi's classifications: the rock tunneling quality index Q (Barton et al. 1974) and the Rock Mass Rating system (Bieniawski 1976/1990). Q can estimate roof pressure and support requirements; RMR relates to stand-up time, unsupported active span, and roof pressure. Both systems are described further in Chapter 1.",
      "body": "Alternate empirical methods for estimating tunnel support pressures are available. The two most common are the rock tunneling quality index, Q, presented in Barton et al. (1974) and the rock mass rating system (e.g., Bieniawski 1976). These systems are described in more detail in Chapter 1. The roof pressure and support requirements for tunnels can be estimated from the value of Q and some of the joint properties. While not solely intended for tunneling applications, the RMR can be related to stand-up time, unsupported active span length, and roof pressure. See Bieniawski (1990) for additional comparison of methods for estimating tunnel support requirements.",
      "key_points": [
        "Two main alternative empirical systems: Q index (Barton et al. 1974) and RMR (Bieniawski 1976/1990).",
        "Q index estimates roof pressure and support requirements using joint properties.",
        "RMR correlates to stand-up time, unsupported active span length, and roof pressure.",
        "Both systems described in detail in Chapter 1; Bieniawski (1990) provides method comparison.",
        "These methods complement Terzaghi's Table 4-6 approach and cross-reference via Table 4-7."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-7"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when using Q index or RMR classification systems as alternative approaches to estimate rock tunnel support pressures."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.4",
      "title": "Openings in Soft Ground (Soil)",
      "summary": "Container section covering the analysis of tunnels in soft ground (soil), including ground behavior classification, support load determination, and surface settlement due to ground loss. Addresses stand-up time, stability factors, arching in coarse-grained soils, undrained behavior in clays, and simplified versus detailed support load selection methods.",
      "body": "",
      "key_points": [
        "Soft ground tunneling analysis includes ground behavior classification, support load selection, and settlement prediction.",
        "Stand-up time is the key parameter governing tunnel support requirements in soft ground.",
        "Arching occurs in coarse-grained soils; undrained shear strength governs in clay soils.",
        "Tables 4-8 through 4-12 provide a systematic framework for classifying behavior and selecting design loads."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when designing or analyzing tunnels excavated through soft ground (soil) conditions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.4.1",
      "title": "Ground Behavior",
      "summary": "Classifies soft ground tunneling behavior by stand-up time and soil type using Terzaghi's Tunnelman's Ground Classification (Table 4-8). Introduces the undrained stability factor Ncrit (Eq. 4-10) for fine-grained soils. Tables 4-9 and 4-10 extend classification to clayey fine-grained soils/silty sands and coarse-grained soils, respectively. Six behavior types are described: firm, raveling, running, flowing, squeezing, and swelling.",
      "body": "Selection of an appropriate method for tunnel construction in soft ground (i.e., soil tunneling) depends upon the response of the soil during and after excavation. This response is often referred to in terms of stand-up time, which is the amount of time the soil will support itself prior to the installation of tunnel supports. The stand-up time depends on the type of soil, the position of groundwater, and the size of the opening. Terzaghi's (1950) Tunnelman's Ground Classification provides a commonly used means of describing various types of ground response. The types of ground behavior for tunneling are summarized in Table 4-8.\n\nIn addition to the descriptions provided in Table 4-8, the behavior of fine-grained soils and silty sands above the water table can be evaluated using Table 4-9. The undrained stability factor (Ncrit) is used to assess the ground behavior as suggested by Peck (1969). This factor is defined as:\n\nNcrit = (σ'v − σt) / su\n\nwhere:\nσ'v = effective overburden pressure at the tunnel centerline,\nσt = interior applied pressure from compressed air or breasting, and\nsu = undrained shear strength.\n\nFor coarse-grained soils, the ground behavior depends on the grain-size distribution, relative density, and the amount of clayey fines (binder) as indicated in Table 4-10. Uniform, loose materials (Cu < 3 and N < 10) with round grains run more freely than well-graded, dense materials (Cu > 6 and N > 30) with angular particles. Soils with properties between those listed in Table 4-10 will tend to exhibit intermediate ground behavior. Very high flowrates should be expected in tunnels below the water table through soils with relatively large particles, such as gravel and medium to coarse sand.",
      "key_points": [
        "Stand-up time governs tunnel support selection; depends on soil type, groundwater position, and opening size.",
        "Six ground behavior types: firm, raveling (slow/fast), running, flowing, squeezing, and swelling (Table 4-8).",
        "Undrained stability factor Ncrit = (σ'v − σt) / su used to assess fine-grained soil behavior (Eq. 4-10).",
        "Table 4-9 classifies clayey fine-grained soil and silty sand behavior using Ncrit values.",
        "Table 4-10 classifies coarse-grained soil behavior based on grain size, relative density (SPT N), and groundwater.",
        "Loose, uniform, round-grained soils (Cu < 3, N < 10) run more freely than dense, well-graded, angular soils.",
        "Gravel and medium-to-coarse sand below water table should be expected to exhibit very high flow rates."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-10",
          "description": "Undrained stability factor Ncrit = (σ'v − σt) / su, where σ'v is effective overburden pressure at tunnel centerline, σt is interior applied pressure (compressed air or breasting), and su is undrained shear strength.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.table_4_9_tunnel_behavior"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-8",
        "Table 4-9",
        "Table 4-10"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when classifying soft ground tunneling behavior to select appropriate construction methods and support systems."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.4.2",
      "title": "Soft Ground Support Loads",
      "summary": "Describes the factors governing support pressures in soft ground tunnels and the loading mechanism (arching in coarse-grained soils; undrained strength in clays). Provides two levels of guidance: a simplified approach (Table 4-11) and a detailed method (Table 4-12) for selecting tunnel support design loads based on ground behavior type determined from Tables 4-8 through 4-10.",
      "body": "Support pressures on tunnels in soft ground are governed by many factors, including the unit weight of the overlying material, the groundwater level, soil properties, the amount deformation allowed during excavation, the interaction between soil and the supports, the opening shape, and the length of time between excavation and lining installation. Other factors should also be considered include the presence of other nearby openings, superimposed loads from neighboring structures, and the possibility of changes in groundwater conditions.\n\nFigure 4-11 illustrates the loading mechanism surrounding soft ground tunnels. In coarse-grained soils, arching occurs above the tunnel. Arching transfers some of the overburden load to the surrounding ground so that only a portion of the total load above the tunnel is applied to the tunnel. In clay soils, undrained conditions tend to control. In this case, the undrained shear strength can be considered to provide support of a portion of the load above the tunnel.\n\nA simplified approach to the selection of tunnel support loads is provided in Table 4-11 (FHWA 2009). More detailed guidance for the selection of tunnel support loads is summarized in Table 4-12. These tables are used by first determining the ground behavior type using Table 4-8 to Table 4-10.",
      "key_points": [
        "Support pressures depend on unit weight, groundwater level, soil properties, deformation allowed, opening shape, and time to lining installation.",
        "Arching in coarse-grained soils reduces load applied to tunnel support.",
        "In clay soils, undrained shear strength governs and provides partial load support.",
        "Table 4-11 provides a simplified design load thickness Hp by ground behavior type for circular and horseshoe tunnels.",
        "Table 4-12 provides detailed support loads for H > 1.5·(B+Ht) covering running, flowing, raveling, squeezing, and swelling ground.",
        "Ground behavior type must first be determined from Tables 4-8 through 4-10 before selecting design loads."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-11"
      ],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-11",
        "Table 4-12"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when selecting design support loads for tunnels in soft ground based on ground behavior classification and tunnel geometry."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.4.3",
      "title": "Loss of Ground",
      "summary": "Explains how underground excavation causes surrounding ground to move toward the opening due to stress release and physical soil loss (raveling, runs, flows), leading to surface settlement. Notes that stress-reduction-related ground loss can be predicted reasonably well, while loss from raveling/runs/flows requires detailed subsurface knowledge. References FHWA (2009) for settlement prediction methods.",
      "body": "As underground excavation is made, the surrounding ground starts to move toward the opening. These displacements occur as the soil around the opening expands due to stress release in addition to soil lost to the tunnel from raveling, runs, flows, etc. The resulting loss of ground causes settlement of the ground surface. The loss of ground associated with stress reduction can be predicted reasonably well, but the ground loss due to raveling, runs, flows, etc. requires a detailed knowledge of the subsurface conditions to avoid unacceptable amounts of settlement. A summary of methods to predict surface settlement resulting from lost ground can be found in FHWA (2009).",
      "key_points": [
        "Ground moves toward an opening due to stress release expansion and physical soil loss (raveling, runs, flows).",
        "Ground loss causes surface settlement above the tunnel.",
        "Stress-reduction-related ground loss is reasonably predictable; raveling/flow-related loss requires detailed subsurface knowledge.",
        "FHWA (2009) provides methods for predicting surface settlement from ground loss."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when evaluating potential surface settlement resulting from ground loss during soft ground tunnel excavation."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.5",
      "title": "Pressure on Vertical Shafts",
      "summary": "Container section covering earth pressure calculations for vertical shafts in both coarse-grained and cohesive soils. In contrast to elastic stress methods in Section 4-3, vertical shaft pressures represent active or passive limiting (plastic) states. Subsections cover coarse-grained soil (active pressures, horizontal arching, λ coefficient) and clay (critical depth zcrit below which support is needed, Eq. 4-11).",
      "body": "In contrast to the methods presented in Section 4-3, the stress calculations for vertical shafts represent either active or passive earth pressure. These limiting earth pressure conditions correspond to a plastic rather than elastic state of stress within the soil.",
      "key_points": [
        "Vertical shaft pressures are active or passive (plastic state), not elastic as in Section 4-3.",
        "Active conditions develop during shaft excavation; passive conditions occur when structure walls push into soil.",
        "Horizontal arching in deep shafts (depth > 2 diameters) reduces active pressures compared to plane strain."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when calculating earth pressures on the walls of vertical cylindrical shafts in any soil type."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.5.1",
      "title": "Shafts in Coarse-Grained Soil",
      "summary": "Addresses active and passive earth pressure for vertical cylindrical shafts in coarse-grained soil. For shallow shafts (depth ≤ 2 diameters), theoretical solutions apply; at greater depths, horizontal arching (coefficient λ = ratio of circumferential to vertical stress) becomes significant. References Cheng et al. (2007) for active pressure solutions plotted in Figure 4-12 for λ = 1 and λ = K0. Notes that rigid top bracing may require at-rest pressure design.",
      "body": "During excavation of a vertical cylindrical shaft in coarse-grained soil, the horizontal pressures around the shaft approach active earth pressure values. If outward-directed forces from a structure (e.g., buried silo) move the structure walls into the soil, the earth pressures will approach passive conditions. Earth pressures are discussed in more detail in DM 7.2.\n\nActive earth pressures for cylindrical shafts have been determined using analytical, limit equilibrium, slip line, numerical, and experimental methods. The active earth pressure coefficient depends on the shaft dimensions and the soil strength. For shallow shafts (i.e., depth ≤ two diameters), theoretical solutions tend to be applicable while the effects of horizontal arching become significant at greater depths (Tobar and Meguid 2010). Horizontal arching is taken into account in some solutions by the coefficient λ, which is the ratio of the circumferential stress to the vertical stress. The value of λ is equal to 1 in analytical solutions, such as Terzaghi (1943) but may be as low as K0. Cheng et al.'s (2007) solution is plotted in Figure 4-12 for λ = 1 and λ = K0.\n\nActive pressures must be modified if rigid bracing at the top of the shaft prevents development of an active state. For restrained vertical shafts, horizontal pressures may be as large as the at-rest pressure on a long wall with plane strain conditions.",
      "key_points": [
        "Excavation of vertical shaft in coarse-grained soil → horizontal pressures approach active values.",
        "Outward structural forces → pressures approach passive conditions.",
        "Shallow shafts (depth ≤ 2 diameters): theoretical solutions applicable; deeper shafts: horizontal arching significant.",
        "λ = circumferential stress / vertical stress accounts for horizontal arching; ranges from 1 (Terzaghi) to as low as K0.",
        "Cheng et al. (2007) solution plotted in Figure 4-12 for λ = 1 and λ = K0.",
        "Rigid top bracing prevents active state development; design for at-rest (plane strain) pressures in that case."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-12"
      ],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when estimating horizontal earth pressures on vertical cylindrical shafts excavated in coarse-grained (granular) soils."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-5.5.2",
      "title": "Shafts in Clay",
      "summary": "Provides the formula for critical depth zcrit (Eq. 4-11) above which no support is needed for vertical shafts in clay: zcrit = 2su / γt, where su is undrained shear strength and γt is total unit weight. Below this depth, lateral support is required. This is analogous to the tension crack depth concept in clay retaining wall design.",
      "body": "No support is needed from the ground surface to a depth of zcrit for shafts in clay. The critical depth (zcrit) is:\n\nzcrit = 2su / γt\n\nwhere:\nsu = the undrained shear strength of the soil and\nγt = the total unit weight of the soil.",
      "key_points": [
        "No lateral support required from ground surface down to critical depth zcrit in clay.",
        "Critical depth: zcrit = 2su / γt (Eq. 4-11).",
        "su = undrained shear strength; γt = total unit weight of soil.",
        "Below zcrit, lateral support must be provided for the shaft walls."
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "id": "Eq. 4-11",
          "description": "Critical depth for unsupported shaft walls in clay: zcrit = 2su / γt, where su is undrained shear strength and γt is total unit weight of soil.",
          "implemented_in": "geotech_references.dm7_1.chapter4.shaft_critical_depth_clay"
        }
      ],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when determining the depth to which a vertical shaft in clay does not require lateral support."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6",
      "title": "Numerical Solutions for Stresses in Soil",
      "summary": "Introduces the use of numerical methods and computer programs for evaluating stresses in soil, noting that analytical and chart-based solutions are limited by their assumptions and struggle with complex subsurface profiles. Positions numerical analysis as an essential complement to classical elastic solutions in modern geotechnical practice.",
      "body": "The analytical and chart-based solutions presented in this chapter are an excellent starting point for evaluation of stresses within a soil mass. However, their applicability is limited by the constraints and assumptions of each. These methods also struggle to effectively model complex subsurface profiles and loading conditions.\n\nComputer programs and numerical solutions are an important part of geotechnical engineering practice. This section provides a brief overview of the application of numerical methods to the evaluation of stresses.",
      "key_points": [
        "Analytical and chart-based solutions are good starting points but are limited by their assumptions and cannot easily model complex conditions.",
        "Numerical methods and computer programs are essential tools in modern geotechnical engineering for stress evaluation.",
        "This section gives a brief overview of numerical approaches including elastic, nonlinear, and finite element methods."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when evaluating stresses in soil masses where analytical/chart solutions are insufficient due to complex geometry or loading."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.1",
      "title": "Numerical Analysis Types",
      "summary": "Surveys the main types of numerical analysis tools used in geotechnical stress evaluation: programs based directly on elastic solutions (e.g., Settle3D, CONSOL, SETOFF) for consolidation settlement, and continuum-based methods such as finite element and finite difference techniques. Highlights that FEA is most popular for stress analysis, with stress calculations being more straightforward than deformation calculations.",
      "body": "Some computer programs (e.g., Settle3D, CONSOL, SETOFF) are available that directly rely upon elastic solutions, such as those presented in this chapter, to calculate changes in stress. These programs are specifically formulated for the solution of consolidation settlement problems discussed in the next chapter. The benefit provided by these programs is the automated calculation of changes in stress and the ability to consider time-dependent changes. However, the solutions depend on the same assumptions as the elastic solutions on which they are based.\n\nMany continuum-based numerical analysis techniques are available. The most common of these in geotechnical engineering are the finite element and finite difference methods, with the former being somewhat more popular for stress analysis problems. These approaches divide the soil or rock into elements or grid points. The relationships between the external and gravitational forces acting on the elements (or grid) and the corresponding displacements are defined using constitutive (stress-strain) laws and failure criteria.\n\nThe following sections provide a condensed overview focused on the calculation of stresses using the finite element method. The use of finite element analysis (FEA) to determine stresses is relatively straight-forward. Calculation of accurate deformations requires significantly more expertise and experience. For a practical introduction to the use of FEA in geotechnical engineering see Bradley and VandenBerge (2015). A more in-depth perspective can be found in the books by Potts and Zdravkovic (1999, 2001).",
      "key_points": [
        "Programs like Settle3D, CONSOL, and SETOFF use elastic solutions directly and are tailored for consolidation settlement problems.",
        "Finite element and finite difference methods are the most common continuum-based approaches; FEA is more popular for stress analysis.",
        "FEA stress calculations are relatively straightforward; accurate deformation predictions require significantly more expertise.",
        "Constitutive (stress-strain) laws and failure criteria define the force-displacement relationships in numerical models.",
        "Key references: Bradley and VandenBerge (2015) for practical FEA; Potts and Zdravkovic (1999, 2001) for in-depth treatment."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when selecting an appropriate numerical analysis tool or method for geotechnical stress or settlement problems."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.2",
      "title": "Linear Elastic Stress Analysis",
      "summary": "Describes the simplest FE analysis approach using linear elastic constitutive theory, requiring only elastic modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν. Notes that with a single material, calculated stresses are independent of E; with multiple materials, relative E values influence stresses due to arching effects. This is the baseline approach for stress prediction in FEA.",
      "body": "The simplest FE analyses use linear elastic constitutive theory, which relates changes in stress linearly to strains (or displacements). For a linear elastic analysis, the only required material parameters are the elastic modulus, E, and Poisson's ratio, ν (or any other two elastic constants such as shear modulus or bulk modulus). For problems with only one material type (and one value of E), the calculated stresses will be independent of the value of E selected. However, when more than one material type is present, the relative values of E assigned to each material in the analysis can impact the calculated stresses because of arching and similar phenomena.",
      "key_points": [
        "Linear elastic FEA requires only two material parameters: elastic modulus E and Poisson's ratio ν.",
        "For single-material problems, calculated stresses are independent of the value of E selected.",
        "With multiple material types, relative E values influence stress distribution due to arching and similar phenomena.",
        "Linear elastic theory is the simplest and most accessible starting point for FE stress analysis."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when performing FEA stress analysis using the simplest constitutive model, especially for single-material or preliminary analyses."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.3",
      "title": "Nonlinear Elastic Stress Analysis",
      "summary": "Covers nonlinear constitutive models for soil stress analysis, focusing on the Duncan-Chang hyperbolic model. Explains the model's basis in observed soil behavior, its key parameters (K, n, Rf, Kb, m, Kur), and notes that linear elastic analysis typically yields major principal stresses within 10% of nonlinear results for embankments. Requires more input parameters and expertise than linear elastic analysis.",
      "body": "The stress-strain behavior of geological materials is truly linear over only a small range of strains. A properly selected nonlinear constitutive model will provide a more accurate prediction of behavior but will require additional input parameters and expertise. VandenBerge et al. (2014) found that major principal effective stresses calculated for embankments with linear elastic analysis were typically within 10% of the values calculated using more rigorous and time-consuming nonlinear procedures.\n\nOne of the earliest and most common nonlinear constitutive theories for soil is the hyperbolic model proposed by Duncan and Chang (1970) and described in more detail in Duncan et al. (1980). This model can also consider stress dependent variations in Poisson's ratio (or bulk modulus). It can be used with either effective stress (drained) or total stress (undrained) problems, provided the model parameters are determined using the appropriate type of test.\n\nThe Duncan-Chang model is based on the following observations:\n• The principal stress difference (σ1 – σ3) tends to vary in a hyperbolic manner with strain,\n• The initial modulus (Ei) increases with increased confining stress in a manner that can be described by a power function,\n• The ultimate value of (σ1 – σ3) predicted by the hyperbola tends to be greater than the value measured by the test,\n• The bulk modulus (Bt) of soil increases with increased confining stress in a manner that can be described by a power function.\n\nSoil tends to respond in a stiffer, approximately linear manner with modulus (Eur) when unloaded and reloaded after some amount of shearing. The required parameters are summarized and illustrated in Table 4-13 and Figure 4-13.",
      "key_points": [
        "Geological materials are linear elastic only over a small strain range; nonlinear models are more accurate but need more parameters.",
        "Linear elastic FEA typically yields major principal stresses within 10% of nonlinear results for embankments (VandenBerge et al. 2014).",
        "The Duncan-Chang hyperbolic model is one of the earliest and most widely used nonlinear constitutive models for soil.",
        "Principal stress difference (σ1 – σ3) varies hyperbolically with strain; initial modulus Ei and bulk modulus Bt increase with confining stress via power functions.",
        "The model applies to both drained (effective stress) and undrained (total stress) conditions with appropriately determined parameters.",
        "Soil exhibits stiffer, approximately linear response with modulus Eur during unload-reload cycles.",
        "Key model parameters: K, n, Rf, Kb, m, Kur — summarized in Table 4-13 and Figure 4-13."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [
        "Figure 4-13"
      ],
      "tables": [
        "Table 4-13"
      ],
      "applicability": "Applies when performing nonlinear FE stress analysis of soil, particularly for embankments or problems where stress-dependent stiffness is important."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.4",
      "title": "Numerical Modeling Best Practice",
      "summary": "Provides mesh and element guidelines for achieving consistent and accurate stress predictions in finite element analysis, based on VandenBerge et al. (2014). Recommends isoparametric elements, uniform meshes, appropriate aspect ratios, small element sizes (1–2% of domain height), and inclusion of boundary water pressures from impounded water.",
      "body": "Both linear and nonlinear finite element analysis can be used to make accurate predictions of stresses. The following guidelines have been found to yield the most consistent and uniform stress predictions (VandenBerge et al. 2014).\n\n• Use isoparametric elements (i.e., 8-noded rectangles or 6-node triangles).\n• Use a uniform mesh with elements of approximately the same shape, especially within any zones of interest.\n• Use elements with an appropriate aspect ratio, preferably longest to shortest dimension, less than or equal to five.\n• Keep the element size as small as practical with respect to the overall dimensions. A maximum element height of approximately 1 to 2% of the height of the problem domain is preferred.\n• Remember to include the boundary water pressures from any impounded water present in the model.",
      "key_points": [
        "Use isoparametric elements: 8-noded rectangles or 6-node triangles for best stress accuracy.",
        "Maintain a uniform mesh, especially in zones of interest, to ensure consistent stress predictions.",
        "Element aspect ratio (longest to shortest dimension) should be ≤ 5.",
        "Maximum element height should be approximately 1–2% of the overall problem domain height.",
        "Include boundary water pressures from any impounded water in the model."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when setting up a finite element model for stress analysis in soil to ensure numerical accuracy and consistency."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.4.1",
      "title": "Initial or Geostatic Stresses",
      "summary": "Explains how initial (geostatic) stresses are established in FE models, including how gravity loading governs vertical stresses and how horizontal stresses develop toward K0 conditions. Covers the relationship K0 = ν/(1-ν) for linear elastic models, the need to zero out gravity-induced displacements before applying new loads, and how to model overconsolidated (K0 > 1.0) conditions via load-unload cycles.",
      "body": "The initial stress state in a FE model is dependent on the process used to \"turn on\" gravity and stress within the model. Vertical stresses are governed mostly by gravity loading. As the mesh deforms in response to gravity, horizontal stresses will develop and tend toward at rest (K0) conditions for a level, laterally-constrained mesh. For a linear elastic model, the value of K0 will be equal to ν / (1-ν). Calculation of initial horizontal stresses in this manner will yield correct results, and K0 will be less than 1.0, which is always the case for primary loading. The calculation of initial horizontal stresses in this way will lead to initial deformation of the model and the layer thicknesses may no longer match the in situ conditions. These displacements are a numerical artifact and should be zeroed or removed prior to examining the effects of new loading conditions. Overconsolidated conditions with K0 greater than 1.0 can be modelled by loading and then unloading the model, following the process by which K0 > 1.0 conditions occur in nature.\n\nIn many cases, the details of the initial stress process are program specific. The engineer using FEA should become familiar with the various options available so that appropriate methods are applied.",
      "key_points": [
        "Vertical stresses in a FE model are governed primarily by gravity loading.",
        "For a linear elastic model, K0 = ν / (1-ν); this is always less than 1.0 under primary loading.",
        "Gravity-induced displacements are numerical artifacts and must be zeroed before evaluating new loading conditions.",
        "Overconsolidated conditions (K0 > 1.0) are modeled by simulating a load-then-unload cycle.",
        "Initial stress procedures are often program-specific; engineers should understand the options in their software."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when initializing geostatic stresses in a FE model, including both normally consolidated and overconsolidated soil conditions."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.4.2",
      "title": "Staged Construction or Stress History",
      "summary": "Describes how FEA models construction sequencing and geologic stress history by adding and subtracting elements in stages. Staged modeling is essential for nonlinear analysis because material properties change with stress level, and is required for predicting displacement patterns even in linear elastic analysis. Required for excavations, tunnels, and similar problems.",
      "body": "Finite element analysis allows the sequence of loading and construction staging to be modeled numerically. To a lesser extent, geologic stress history can also be modeled. Within the model, this occurs by adding and subtracting elements (or their weight) in steps. Staged models are especially important for nonlinear analysis because the properties of the material change with stress level.\n\nFor example, a staged approach is required to predict stresses and displacements around open excavations and tunnels. The initial ground conditions should be modeled after which the effects of removing soil or rock can be considered.\n\nFor cases where only stress distributions are required, such staging is unnecessary in linear elastic analysis. The use of a staged model is required, even for linear elasticity, to predict correct patterns of displacement.",
      "key_points": [
        "FEA can model construction staging and, to a lesser extent, geologic stress history by adding/subtracting elements in steps.",
        "Staged modeling is critical for nonlinear analysis because material stiffness changes with stress level.",
        "Staged analysis is required to predict stresses and displacements around excavations and tunnels.",
        "For linear elastic stress-only analysis, staging is not required; but staging is needed for correct displacement patterns even in linear elastic models."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when modeling construction sequences, excavations, tunnels, or geologic stress history in FEA, especially with nonlinear constitutive models."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.5",
      "title": "Evaluation of Stress Due to Applied Loads",
      "summary": "Explains how to evaluate changes in stress from applied loads using staged FE models: establish initial in situ stress state in early stages, then apply new loads (distributed, point, or new soil layers) and compare stresses between stages. Covers the applicability of 2D (plane strain) vs. 3D analysis and the need to validate FE results against analytical solutions.",
      "body": "Changes in stress due to applied loads can be evaluated using a staged FE model. The first stage(s) of the model are used to create the desired initial state of stress that best represents the in situ conditions. At this point, the new loading can be added to the model in various forms, including distributed loads, point loads, and new soil layers. The predicted changes in stress can then be evaluated by comparing the predicted stress at convenient points within the model between subsequent stages. Where possible, the changes in stress predicted by FE models should be checked with analytical solutions such as those presented earlier in this chapter.\n\nEngineers should be aware of the limitations of their numerical analyses. For example, two-dimensional FEA are useful for predicting changes in stress below long foundations, embankments, and large area fills because such problems can be analyzed in a plane strain manner. However, a three-dimensional program would be required to predict changes in stress below more complex conditions, such as a rectangular foundation.",
      "key_points": [
        "Use staged FE models: early stages establish in situ stress; later stages introduce new loads and evaluate stress changes.",
        "New loads can be applied as distributed loads, point loads, or new soil layers.",
        "Stress changes are evaluated by comparing results between consecutive stages at points of interest.",
        "FE-predicted stress changes should be cross-checked against analytical solutions wherever possible.",
        "2D plane strain FEA is appropriate for long foundations, embankments, and large fills; 3D analysis is needed for rectangular foundations and complex geometries."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when using staged FEA to evaluate stress changes beneath applied loads such as foundations, embankments, or fills."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-6.6",
      "title": "Evaluation of Stress within Embankments and Slopes",
      "summary": "Addresses the added complexity of stress evaluation in slopes and embankments, where no closed-form analytical solution exists and K0 conditions do not apply near slope faces. Recommends starting with a level-ground FE model and progressively excavating elements to form the slope, mimicking natural slope formation, potentially requiring multiple analysis stages to establish initial conditions.",
      "body": "The calculation of stresses is more complex for slopes and embankments compared with relatively level ground. First, no closed-form analytical solution is available for comparison because the soil in a slope is not laterally restrained. The lack of lateral restraint also means that K0 conditions will not be present, especially close to the face of a slope.\n\nStresses near natural slopes are best evaluated by starting with level ground in the FE model and progressively removing (excavating) elements to form the slope. Multiple stages of analysis may be required to establish the initial conditions. The removal of elements mimics the process by which the slope was formed in nature.",
      "key_points": [
        "Stress calculation in slopes and embankments is more complex than for level ground — no closed-form analytical solution exists for comparison.",
        "K0 conditions do not apply near slope faces due to lack of lateral restraint.",
        "Best practice: start FE model with level ground and progressively excavate elements to form the slope, mimicking natural slope formation.",
        "Multiple analysis stages may be required to establish correct initial stress conditions for slopes."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when evaluating in situ or initial stresses within natural slopes, embankments, or excavated slopes using FEA."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-7",
      "title": "Suggested Reading",
      "summary": "Provides a curated list of key references for Chapter 4 topics including stress analysis and Mohr circles (Parry 2004), elastic solutions (Poulos & Davis 1974), buried pipe design (Moser 1990), underground openings (FHWA 2009), and numerical stress analysis using finite element methods (Bradley & VandenBerge 2015; Potts & Zdravkovic 2001).",
      "body": "Topic | Reference\nStress and Mohr Circles | Parry, R. H. 2004. Mohr circles, stress paths and geotechnics, CRC Press, 2004.\nElastic Solutions | Poulos, H. G. and Davis, E. H. 1974. Elastic Solutions for Soil and Rock Mechanics, John Wiley & Sons Inc.\nStress on Pipes | Moser, A. P. 1990. Buried Pipe Design, McGraw-Hill Inc., (third edition also available by Moser and Folkman).\nUnderground Openings | FHWA. 2009. Technical Manual for Design and Construction of Road Tunnels – Civil Elements, FHWA-NHI-09-010, Federal Highway Administration.\nNumerical Stress Analysis | Bradley, N. and VandenBerge, D. R. 2015. Beginner's Guide for Geotechnical Finite Element Analyses, CGPR Report No. 82, Center for Geotechnical Practice and Research, Virginia Tech.\n| Potts, D. M. and Zdravkovic, L. 2001. Finite Element Analysis in Geotechnical Engineering: Theory and Application, ICE Publishing.",
      "key_points": [
        "Parry (2004) is the primary reference for Mohr circles, stress paths, and geotechnics.",
        "Poulos & Davis (1974) covers elastic solutions applicable to soil and rock mechanics stress problems.",
        "Moser (1990) and the Moser & Folkman third edition address buried pipe design and stress on pipes.",
        "FHWA (2009) FHWA-NHI-09-010 is the reference for underground opening and road tunnel design.",
        "Bradley & VandenBerge (2015) and Potts & Zdravkovic (2001) cover geotechnical finite element analysis theory and practice."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies when seeking foundational or supplementary references for the stress analysis methods, pipe loading, underground openings, and numerical methods covered in Chapter 4."
    },
    {
      "section_id": "4-8",
      "title": "Notation",
      "summary": "Defines all symbols and notation used throughout Chapter 4, covering geometric parameters (B, Bc, Bd, H, Ht, r, z), soil and material properties (E, Ei, Eur, Bt, Ka, K0, Kp, ν, γt, γʹ, φʹ, su), pipe loading terms (Cd, Wc, Wd, Wp), tunnel parameters (pc, σt, Q, RMR, RQD, Ncrit, zcrit), stress quantities (σ1, σ2, σ3, σh, σv, Δσz), and Duncan-Chang model parameters (Kb, Kur, Rf).",
      "body": "Symbol | Description\nB | Width of a foundation, loaded area, or tunnel\nBc | Diameter of a flexible pipe\nBd | Width of trench in pipe loading calculations\nBt | Bulk modulus of soil\nCd | Load coefficient in pipe loading calculations\nCu | Coefficient of uniformity (from grain-size distribution)\nD | Outer diameter of pipe\nE | Elastic modulus\nEA | Active earth pressure force\nEi | Initial tangent modulus\nEur | Unload-reload modulus for Duncan-Chang model\nhp | Pressure head\nH | Depth of soil cover or vertical distance between ground surface and tunnel roof\nHp | Design load thickness in terms of depth\nHt | Tunnel height\nI | Influence factor for change in stress calculations\nKa | Active earth pressure coefficient\nKb | Bulk modulus parameter for Duncan-Chang model\nK0 | At-rest earth pressure coefficient\nKp | Passive earth pressure coefficient\nKur | Unload-reload modulus parameter Duncan-Chang model\nN | Standard Penetration Test blow count\nNcrit | Undrained stability factor\npc | Tunnel air pressure\npʹf | Center of Mohr circle at failure (MIT stress path space)\nPa | Atmospheric pressure\nu | Pore water pressure\nqf | Radius of Mohr circle at failure\nq0 | Applied pressure or load\nQ | Rock tunneling quality index\nr | Horizontal distance from centerline of a foundation\nRf | Reduction factor for Duncan-Chang model\nRMR | Rock mass rating\nRQD | Rock quality designation\nsu | Undrained shear strength\nt | Stand up time for tunneling in raveling soils\nT | Elapsed time between excavation and completion of permanent structure\nWc | Flexible pipe load\nWd | Rigid pipe load\nWp | Prism load on pipe\nz | Depth below an applied load\nzcrit | Critical depth for unsupported shafts in clay soils\nZi | Soil layer thickness\nα | Angle between the major principal plane and the plane of interest\n∆σz | Change in vertical stress\nφʹ | Effective stress friction angle\nγʹ | Effective unit weight\nγt | Total unit weight\ngw | Unit weight of water\nλ | Ratio of the circumferential stress to the vertical stress\nµʹ | Coefficient of friction for trench backfill\nν | Poisson's ratio\nσ1 | Major principal stress\nσ2 | Intermediate principal stress\nσ3 | Minor principal stress\nσh | Total horizontal stress\nσʹh | Effective horizontal stress\nσt | Interior tunnel pressure from compressed air or breasting\nσv | Total vertical stress\nσʹv | Effective vertical stress",
      "key_points": [
        "Geometric symbols include B (foundation/tunnel width), Bc (flexible pipe diameter), Bd (trench width), H (soil cover depth), Ht (tunnel height), r (horizontal distance from centerline), and z (depth below load).",
        "Soil and material property symbols include E (elastic modulus), Ei (initial tangent modulus), Eur (unload-reload modulus), Bt (bulk modulus of soil), ν (Poisson's ratio), φʹ (effective friction angle), γt (total unit weight), γʹ (effective unit weight), and su (undrained shear strength).",
        "Pipe loading notation includes Cd (load coefficient), Wc (flexible pipe load), Wd (rigid pipe load), and Wp (prism load on pipe).",
        "Tunnel-specific symbols include pc (tunnel air pressure), σt (interior tunnel pressure), Q (rock tunneling quality index), RMR (rock mass rating), RQD (rock quality designation), Ncrit (undrained stability factor), and zcrit (critical depth for unsupported shafts).",
        "Principal and in-situ stress symbols: σ1 (major), σ2 (intermediate), σ3 (minor), σh/σʹh (total/effective horizontal), σv/σʹv (total/effective vertical), and Δσz (change in vertical stress).",
        "Duncan-Chang model parameters are defined: Kb (bulk modulus parameter), Kur (unload-reload modulus parameter), and Rf (reduction factor).",
        "Earth pressure coefficients Ka (active), K0 (at-rest), and Kp (passive) are all defined, along with EA (active earth pressure force)."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "figures": [],
      "tables": [],
      "applicability": "Applies whenever interpreting equations, figures, or tables in Chapter 4 that use symbolic notation for stress analysis, pipe loading, tunnel design, or soil constitutive model parameters."
